/** @page i18ncatalogs Message Catalogs

    \c I18n::MessageCatalog stores schema-versioned native catalogs passed as Qore data. Lookup uses source strings by
    default and supports stable symbolic ids with \c tc(). Locale fallback is deterministic:
    \c fr-CA-u-nu-latn falls back through \c fr-CA, \c fr, and \c root for catalog identity.

    @section i18ncatalogsnative Native Catalog Data

    @code{.py}
%requires i18n

hash<auto> native_catalog = {
    "schema_version": I18n::get_catalog_schema_version(),
    "locales": {
        "root": {"messages": {"Hello, {name}": "Hello, {name}"}},
        "cs": {"messages": {
            "Hello, {name}": {
                "message": "Ahoj, {name}",
                "state": "translated",
            },
            "1 file": {
                "plural_source": "{count} files",
                "forms": {
                    "one": "{count} soubor",
                    "few": "{count} soubory",
                    "other": "{count} souboru",
                },
                "state": "translated",
            },
            "workflow.greeting": {
                "source": "Hello, {name}",
                "message": "Nazdar, {name}",
                "state": "translated",
            },
        }},
    },
};

I18n::MessageCatalog catalog(native_catalog);
printf("%s\n", catalog.t("Hello, {name}", "cs-CZ", {"name": "Petr"}));
printf("%s\n", catalog.tn(2, "1 file", "{count} files", "cs"));
printf("%s\n", catalog.tc("workflow.greeting", "Hello, {name}", "cs", {"name": "Eva"}));
    @endcode

    @section i18ncatalogsjson JSON Catalogs

    The same native shape can be stored as UTF-8 JSON and loaded without evaluating catalog text as Qore code:

    @code{.py}
hash<auto> catalog_data = I18n::load_native_catalog("catalog.json");
I18n::MessageCatalog catalog_from_file("catalog.json");
I18n::MessageCatalog layered_catalog(("base.json", "override.json"));
    @endcode

    File-loading APIs and constructors are tagged with the \c FILESYSTEM functional domain and enforce a bounded file
    size. JSON parsing is strict, rejects duplicate object keys, and validates UTF-8 strings.

    A caller that inspects catalog files before composing them - to enforce its own ownership or collision rules, for
    example - should not let the file-loading constructors read those files a second time. Parse each file once with
    \c I18n::load_native_catalog() and pass the parsed catalogs to the list-of-hashes \c I18n::MessageCatalog
    constructor, which applies exactly the same schema, placeholder, state, and precedence rules as the file
    constructors:

    @code{.py}
list<hash<auto>> native_catalogs = map I18n::load_native_catalog($1), files;
inspect(native_catalogs);
I18n::MessageCatalog catalog(native_catalogs);
    @endcode

    \c I18n::get_catalog_file_parse_count() returns the number of catalog files this process has parsed from disk, so
    a test can assert that composing \c N files performs exactly \c N parses rather than inferring it from elapsed
    time.

    @section i18ncatalogsdiscovery Discovery

    Discovery uses the configured system catalog directory by default.  \c QORE_CATALOG_DIR replaces that default,
    and an explicit \c search_path option replaces both. Returned files are already ordered for deterministic
    composition: lower precedence first, with \c root before more-specific locale files. Applications and modules can
    contribute independently to one domain by installing owner fragments as
    \c domain/locale/owner.json; fragments are composed in deterministic filename order after the direct catalog for
    the same domain and locale.

    Because every discovered fragment is composed, an installation must not keep fragments that its current sources no
    longer produce: an app that moves from one owner module to another would otherwise be described by both owners at
    once, and composition rejects conflicting definitions of the same message id. Projects installing catalogs with
    \c QORE_INSTALL_USER_MODULE_CATALOGS() therefore call \c QORE_FINALIZE_CATALOG_INSTALL() once, after all such
    calls, which reconciles each catalog root at install time against a per-project manifest kept in the
    \c .qore-catalog-manifests directory below that root, and against the owner names of the modules it installs
    catalogs for -- a fragment is named after the module that owns it, so fragments left by an earlier release of the
    same module are retired even on the first such install. Only the fragments belonging to the same project are ever
    removed, so several projects can keep contributing to one shared catalog root.

    @code{.py}
list<string> files = I18n::discover_catalog_files("billing", "cs-CZ", {
    "search_path": ("/opt/qore/catalogs",),
    "override_path": "/etc/qore/catalogs",
});
I18n::MessageCatalog billing_catalog(files);
    @endcode

    Applications do not need a source-code locale allowlist. \c I18n::discover_catalog_locales() enumerates the
    canonical locale identities that actually have direct, flat, or fragment catalogs for a domain. It ignores
    incidental files, invalid or noncanonical locale names, and empty fragment directories:

    @code{.py}
list<string> locales = I18n::discover_catalog_locales("billing", {
    "search_path": ("/opt/qore/catalogs",),
    "override_path": "/etc/qore/catalogs",
});
    @endcode

    Every \c I18n::discover_catalog_files() call reads the filesystem: it probes the candidate paths and scans the
    fragment directory for each search root and locale-fallback entry. An operation that discovers many domains or
    locales at once therefore reads the same directories repeatedly. \c I18n::CatalogDiscoveryIndex answers those
    reads from a snapshot instead, reading each directory at most once and returning exactly what
    \c I18n::discover_catalog_files() returns for the same arguments:

    @code{.py}
I18n::CatalogDiscoveryIndex index();
foreach string domain in (domains) {
    foreach string locale in (index.discoverLocales(domain, opts)) {
        I18n::MessageCatalog catalog(index.discover(domain, locale, opts));
    }
}
    @endcode

    An index is never refreshed and has no invalidation: how stale its view of the catalog tree can become is
    bounded by the lifetime of the object, so it belongs to one operation and must not be kept as a process-global
    cache, where catalogs installed or removed after it was created would stay invisible or keep being reported.
    \c CatalogDiscoveryIndex::clear() takes a new snapshot with the same object, and
    \c CatalogDiscoveryIndex::getDirectoryScanCount() reports how many directories were actually read. Access
    control is unchanged: a directory a sandbox manager does not allow the program to read as a whole is probed path
    by path exactly as it is without an index, so an index never changes which catalog files a program can discover.

    Runtime state policy follows gettext's conservative behavior by default: \c fuzzy, \c needs-review, and
    \c stale entries are stored but not used at runtime. Pass \c {"use_unreviewed": True} to the constructor to preview
    those entries in development.

    Catalog construction validates placeholders. Plain translations must preserve exactly the placeholders from the
    source text. Plural forms must preserve all non-\c count source placeholders and may use \c count for the selected
    numeric value. Message formatting fails if a required placeholder argument is missing. Placeholder output is
    wrapped in Unicode bidi isolation marks automatically for RTL requested locales such as Arabic, Hebrew, Persian,
    and Urdu.

    Catalogs for producer-owned presentation metadata or other opaque prose can pass
    \c {"literal_messages": True}. In this mode, braces in non-plural messages are ordinary text, placeholder
    validation and interpolation are disabled, and translations and source fallbacks are returned unchanged.
    Plural entries keep the normal placeholder rules because plural selection can interpolate \c count.
*/
